WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.050
- So this may sound weird

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:03.420
but I promise it'll start
to make sense eventually.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.100
So I sort of observed the other day

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.410
that there are two different
ways that people watch movies.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.860
The first group of people,
they get home from work,

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.130
they're pretty tired so
they sit on the couch.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.130
They have a Netflix subscription,
so they throw a movie on.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.950
Doesn't really matter what it is

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.690
as long as it's sort of stimulating.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.700
There'll be on their phone,
there'll be eating something,

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.490
and they're just, sort of,
they have the movie on.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:25.820
It's just there for them.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.450
Group number two operates
completely differently.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.120
When they watch a movie
it's a lot more intentional.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.920
Maybe they looked up some reviews on IMDB,

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.710
or maybe they heard from a friend

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.100
that was supposed to be really good.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.300
So they make a night out of it.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.060
They invite some friends over

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.340
or they just watch it
themselves with headphones in.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.980
But they're actively
engaged the whole time.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.150
I call this active movie watching.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.180
Now, being the film nerd that I am,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.220
I fall into group number two.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.410
I very rarely just put on a movie

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.340
for the sake of putting on a movie.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.740
I really like film and
I'm really engaged in it.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.050
And I like the formalistic quality of it.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:01.840
Now, as snobbish as this makes me sound,

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.930
the point of this video,
isn't to flex on you

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.780
about how cultured I am,
but to explain a point.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.130
The difference between passive engagement

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.310
and active engagement,
and how getting them wrong

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.420
can actually really damage
your entire lifestyle,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.230
your relationships with others,

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.500
and your work and everything.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.940
Basically, your whole life can be ruined

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.720
if you don't get this right.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.740
Being actively engaged with something

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.710
increases the quality of the
thing that you're engaged with.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.960
Whether it's your work, a conversation,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.120
active engagement is better
almost every single time.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.210
But there's a problem.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.610
Active engagement requires willpower

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.980
and willpower is a finite resource.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.460
So it's difficult, if not impossible,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.130
to be actively engaged
with every single thing

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.970
throughout the entire day,
all day, every single day.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.480
You're gonna completely burnout.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.120
A great example of this is
we all know a guy like Tom.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.320
Tom spends a lot of time at work.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.340
He's doing really well in his career,

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.890
and he's actively engaged
with the problems at work.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:01.950
Work problems.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.550
That's what problems at work are called.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.410
He's trying hard. He's
climbing the ladder.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:07.840
But he also has a family,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.420
and by the time he gets home
he's so burnt out from work

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.830
that he sits on the couch,
passively watching some TV,

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.160
cracks open a beer.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.200
You know, maybe his
wife wants to talk about

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.100
some problems that
she's having in her life

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.530
and he doesn't really, you know,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.350
he's not really listening at all,

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.390
or trying to offer any sort of help.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:26.997
Maybe his son comes up to him and says,

00:02:26.997 --> 00:02:29.400
"Dad, can we play outside?
Can we throw the Frisbee?"

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.810
And he's, you know, "Not today son."

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.020
And sometimes you do need a day like this.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.590
Maybe you can't be super
engaged with your family

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.230
every time you get home from work.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.550
But for a lot of people like
Tom, this is every single day

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.210
and compounded over
weeks, months, and years,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.610
this can really put a strain

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.560
on the relationships in your life.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.740
So perhaps you can relate to
Tom in your own kind of way.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.500
Maybe you're spending a lot of energy

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.550
on things that you
don't really care about,

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:55.860
things that you don't enjoy,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.280
and things that you find
ultimately unfulfilling,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.160
which leaves you with next to no energy

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.500
for the things that are
actually important in your life.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.900
So what's the solution?

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.740
Do you just quit your job

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.860
and sit in your house
in a meditative state,

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.070
waiting for your wife
and kids to come home?

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:15.190
The solution is to become better
at mental energy management

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.320
or MEM.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.760
So here are just a couple of ways

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.920
to better manage your mental energy.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.860
Number one is to take a
survey of your typical day.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:23.820
What do you do at work?

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:25.700
What are your tasks at work?

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:26.990
What do you do when you get home?

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.210
And then figure out how much attention

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.430
each of those things really needs?

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.210
So obviously, there are things at work

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.640
that actually do require
your full attention.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.046
Especially if you have
a job that has a lot

00:03:37.046 --> 00:03:38.770
of creative problem-solving.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.080
But most jobs also have areas

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.990
which don't require that much energy,

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:46.960
like writing an email or
answering a phone call,

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.170
sweeping the floors.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.760
Things that you can really sort of

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.160
mentally disengage from.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:52.630
Write all that stuff down.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.650
Figure out what things are important,

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.050
what things you can engage with,

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:57.730
and what things you can disengage with.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.250
But that's sort of the precursor

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.020
to the actual tips that I
have, and I have four of them.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.380
So tip number one is to take
more restorative breaks.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:06.830
One thing that I've noticed is that

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.740
when I'm actively engaged
with my most important work,

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.300
say I am video editing a very creative,

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.690
say it's like a wedding
video or a corporate video,

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:17.500
I pretty much have to be all
there when I'm doing that.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.210
But when I take a break,
I make a critical mistake,

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:22.250
and a lot of the time I just browse Reddit

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.470
or I watch YouTube or I
do something like that.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.260
I go on social media on my phone.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.360
And that continues to
work away at my brain.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.330
It's not actually restorative.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.390
I'm still mentally engaged
with whatever's in front of me.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.960
And it's taxing. It's
not actually relaxing.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.550
What I should be doing
is going for a walk,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.380
meditating, taking a nap,
maybe making myself a snack.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.050
All of these things cause you to unplug

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.190
and it sort of heals your brain in a way.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.120
Things like browsing Reddit
keeps your mind busy,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.240
and by the end of the day,
you're gonna be so pooped out

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.080
all you're gonna wanna do is be a zombie

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.120
in front of a TV and breathe air.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.790
So tip number two is to force yourself

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.050
to physically and mentally relax

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.970
during less important tasks.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.440
Now, this one sounds kind of weird

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.340
but it actually works really well.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.980
So say you have something like

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.960
a menial email that
you're supposed to write

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:13.930
for an unimportant client,

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.450
or say you're sweeping
the floors or something,

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:19.200
or making a sandwich,
whatever your job is.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.270
It's the part of the job

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.220
that doesn't require a lot of thought.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.830
What you should do is relax
your body when you're doing it.

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.310
Relax your body and mind

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:31.100
and sort of ease, float, be free. (laughs)

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.490
Now, this may sound like a
little bit of woo-woo crap,

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.990
but it's certainly not.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.280
Listen to me. Believe me.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.170
Actually, you don't have to
believe me if you don't want.

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.700
But what you can't deny
is that our physiology

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.240
and psychology are intimately linked

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.740
in ways we can't even
fully comprehend yet.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.320
What affects the body often
affects the mind and vice versa.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:51.830
When you relax in your seat,

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.580
when you are just a little bit more

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.420
lackadaisical in your posture,

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.130
you can mentally relax as well.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:58.990
So if you're stressed out writing an email

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.900
and you're angry at the client,
and you're really rigid,

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.210
that's gonna mentally wear you out so fast

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.610
you don't even realize it.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.430
Instead, when you're
doing less important stuff

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:09.850
that doesn't require too
much of your attention,

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:10.790
just take it easy.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.930
Maybe play some spa music.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.550
Get someone lower down in the company

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.080
to give you a little foot rub.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.020
And just, you know, be a douche.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.960
It's not that hard of work.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.980
So don't treat it like hard work.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.810
Treat it like a little mini-vacation

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.080
before your actual restorative breaks.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.690
Now tip number three is
probably the most important one.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.660
And that is to be more intentional

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.800
with how actively engaged you are

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.800
with the work that
requires your attention.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.480
So for me, that's video editing.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.530
So when I sit down to
edit, I should be focused.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.370
Like really, really focused.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.390
And I shouldn't break that focus.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.490
Be very, very efficient with my time

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.450
so that it can be over with quicker.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:49.710
And that's the key here.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.780
Being really, really efficient
in the time that you do have

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.560
in your most important work
can get it over with quicker

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.210
so that you have more time to just relax,

00:06:58.210 --> 00:06:59.980
saving mental energy for the things

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:01.490
that are important in your life.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:05.480
Delegate a parcel of time
that you dedicate fully

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.620
to unadulterated productivity.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:08.920
Screw multitasking.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.200
I'm talking about diving into one thing

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:14.850
with your full attention and
being maximally effective.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.650
If you learn to do that
for like two or three hours

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.930
and you enter this deep workspace,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.510
you can get more done than
you ever knew possible.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:24.420
Not only that, but it's over with quicker.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.290
So the rest of your day can be a breeze.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:29.010
Now, tip number four. And this
is kind of the obvious one.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.710
But it's barely a tip.
It's more like a reminder.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:33.880
And that is just to take
care of your health.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:36.170
Obviously, if you're not
getting enough sleep every night

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:38.140
then you're not gonna
have the mental energy

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:41.810
or willpower to focus on the
important things in your life.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:46.220
And mental energy
management just won't work.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:47.370
All right, that's it, guys.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.820
So really take mental
note or a physical note

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.450
as to when you're being
more passively engaged

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:54.320
and when you're being
more actively engaged

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.890
and figure out if it's congruent
with your actual values.

